---
import DefaultLayout from '@layouts/DefaultLayout.astro'
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components'

/**
 * Roadmap: principles, what's shipped, Now / Next / Later lanes and the 1.0 bar.
 * Source of truth for the content: the roadmap thread's working file. Keep issue
 * links current when items land.
 */
---

<DefaultLayout
  title="Roadmap"
  description="Where pywire is heading: what guides it, what's shipped, and what comes Now, Next and Later on the way to 1.0."
>
  <div class="roadmap-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Roadmap</p>
        <h1>Where the <span>wire</span> runs next</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          pywire is heading to 1.0: a stable API you can build a business on, and everything a production SaaS needs
          without a heavy JavaScript frontend. Here's the route, in the order it gets built. Until 1.0, expect breaking
          changes between minor versions.
        </p>
        <div class="legend">
          <span><i class="node now" aria-hidden="true"></i>Now: being built</span>
          <span><i class="node next" aria-hidden="true"></i>Next: planned</span>
          <span><i class="node later" aria-hidden="true"></i>Later: toward 1.0 and beyond</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="principles" aria-labelledby="principles-h">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="eyebrow" id="principles-h">What guides it</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Built for agents</dt><dd>Docs, tests and tooling made for agentic coding.</dd></div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scales by design</dt><dd>Sessions shared across workers, restored on reconnect, minimal diffs.</dd>
          </div>
          <div><dt>Built for SaaS</dt><dd>Real products without a heavy JavaScript frontend.</dd></div>
          <div>
            <dt>Small core, official add-ons</dt><dd>Batteries you opt into, maintained alongside the core.</dd>
          </div>
          <div><dt>Server and edge are equals</dt><dd>Long-running servers and serverless, same code.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Real-time is core</dt><dd>Shared state, presence and rooms ship in 1.0.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>WebTransport ready</dt><dd>Built in, switched on as the server stack catches up.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Open source</dt><dd>Apache 2.0, backed by sponsors.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="shipped" aria-labelledby="shipped-h">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="eyebrow" id="shipped-h">Already shipped</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/docs/concepts/reactivity/">Signals and <code>.wire</code> components</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/routing/">File routing and layouts</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/authentication/">Auth with OIDC and a local IdP</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/scaling/">Redis and Valkey sessions</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/deployment/">Deploys to Docker, Fly, Render, Railway and Cloudflare</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/framework-integration/">Mounting in FastAPI</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/editor-setup/">Language server and VS Code</a></li>
          <li><a href="/docs/guides/cli/"><code>pywire check</code> and the CLI</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="lanes wrap">
      <section class="lane" aria-labelledby="now-h">
        <div class="lane-head">
          <i class="node now" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <h2 id="now-h">Now</h2>
          <p class="sum">Make what already exists trustworthy. Your first real app shouldn't hit a framework bug.</p>
          <p class="top3">
            <strong>First up:</strong> components in loops, shared state for every user, and edge mode. Cards are in priority
            order.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="items">
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Components that behave</h3><p>
              Components inside <code>$for</code> get their own instance, <code>props</code> work in functions defined in
              a component's frontmatter, and child-to-parent callbacks get documented. Plus two fixes from the benchmark run.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 290" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/290" rel="external"
                >#290</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 297" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/297" rel="external"
                >#297</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/332"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 332: @input handler example fails">#332</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/333"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 333: query variable overwritten on edge">#333</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Shared state reaches everyone</h3><p>
              A change to a shared <code>wire()</code> or <code>producer()</code> reaches every connected client, across workers.
              In-process by default, Redis when <code>REDIS_URL</code> is set. Until it lands, the docs say exactly what is
              shared.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 284" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/284" rel="external"
                >#284</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Edge stateless mode</h3><p>
              Run on Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda, Azure Functions and Google Cloud with signed state snapshots. It
              also brings keyed <code>&#123;$for&#125;</code> regions to every tier, so a one-row change on a 5,000-row list
              sends one row over WebSocket (304 B instead of 913 KB), plus optimistic UI and <code>@poll</code>. What
              differs between tiers goes in a published capability table.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <span class="status">In a pull request</span><a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Pull request 282"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/pull/282"
                rel="external">#282</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Faster by default</h3><p>
              Lighter session saves per event, compressed stateless snapshots, gzip and Brotli responses, and published
              benchmarks for the default WebSocket mode.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/328"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 328: per-event session save">#328</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/329"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 329: stateless snapshot size">#329</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/330"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 330: edge list memory">#330</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/331"
                rel="external"
                aria-label="Issue 331: compression">#331</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Production add-ons</h3><p>
              <code>pywire-secure</code> for CSRF, security headers and rate limits. <code>pywire.testing</code> for testing
              pages. <code>pywire-observability</code> for request IDs, JSON logs, OpenTelemetry and Sentry.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <span class="status">In pull requests</span><a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Pull request 261"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/pull/261"
                rel="external">#261</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Pull request 262"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/pull/262"
                rel="external">#262</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Pull request 263"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/pull/263"
                rel="external">#263</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>A tighter dev loop</h3><p>
              Reload on any change under <code>src/</code>, new pages without a restart, and the full traceback when a
              page fails on first render.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 287" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/287" rel="external"
                >#287</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 298" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/298" rel="external"
                >#298</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 300" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/300" rel="external"
                >#300</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>FastAPI, first class</h3><p>
              Mounting pywire in FastAPI already works (<a href="/docs/guides/framework-integration/">guide</a>). Next:
              dependency injection and auth shared with your FastAPI routes, and the rough edges smoothed out.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3><code>pywire check</code> knows the classic mistakes</h3><p>
              Static checks for the template and handler pitfalls people actually hit, reported in the terminal and in
              your editor.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 301" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/301" rel="external"
                >#301</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="lane" aria-labelledby="next-h">
        <div class="lane-head">
          <i class="node next" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <h2 id="next-h">Next</h2>
          <p class="sum">Everything a SaaS needs, from live data to deploys from CI.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="items">
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Real-time between users</h3><p>
              Rooms and channels for chat, presence and collaborative views, plus server-sent events for streaming on
              the stateless tier.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Live data with <code>pywire-db</code></h3><p>
              Queries that re-render every page showing them when the data changes, in every worker. SQLAlchemy and
              SQLModel first, the Django ORM later, and <code>invalidate()</code> for raw SQL.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Forms, done properly</h3><p>
              Typed form refs, declarative validation that reconciles HTML5 and Pydantic, sensible debounce defaults,
              and file uploads documented as a feature.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 19" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/19" rel="external"
                >#19</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 17" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/17" rel="external"
                >#17</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 11" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/11" rel="external"
                >#11</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 16" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/16" rel="external"
                >#16</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 15" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/15" rel="external"
                >#15</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Docs and examples for coding agents</h3><p>
              An <code>AGENTS.md</code> in every new app, an agent skill for building with pywire, guides on structuring complex
              apps, and reference apps that show the patterns.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>A real SaaS starter</h3><p>
              The <code>saas</code> template grows into a reference app with auth, teams, settings, billing hooks and email.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Deploy from CI</h3><p>
              <code>pywire deploy</code> reads config from the environment, prints <code>--json</code> and returns meaningful
              exit codes.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Discussion 283"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions/283"
                rel="external">Discussion #283</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Testing, fuller</h3><p>
              A test client over a real WebSocket, <code>client.login()</code>, events fired from CSS selectors, HTML
              snapshots, and fixtures loaded from <code>.wire</code> files.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/249" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 249"
                >#249</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/250" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 250"
                >#250</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/251" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 251"
                >#251</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/252" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 252"
                >#252</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/253" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 253"
                >#253</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Observability, deeper</h3><p>
              An OpenTelemetry span per event, a metrics endpoint, and traces that survive reconnects.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/254" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 254"
                >#254</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/255" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 255"
                >#255</a
              ><a class="ref" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/256" rel="external" aria-label="Issue 256"
                >#256</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Editors that know your components</h3><p>
              Prop completion, go-to-definition on component tags, and quick fixes from <code>pywire check --fix</code>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>WebTransport in production</h3><p>
              pywire's WebTransport transport is built, with automatic fallback to WebSocket. It turns on by default as
              Hypercorn and Starlette finish HTTP/3 support and hosts forward WebTransport to your app, with middleware
              applied the same as over WebSocket.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Flask and Django</h3><p>Guides for mounting pywire inside an existing Flask or Django app.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Community</h3><p>A contributor guide, good first issues, a Discord, and RFCs in GitHub Discussions.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="lane" aria-labelledby="later-h">
        <div class="lane-head">
          <i class="node later" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <h2 id="later-h">Later</h2>
          <p class="sum">The run-up to 1.0, and a few ideas past it.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="items">
          <li class="item">
            <h3>A stability promise</h3><p>
              Semantic versioning, a deprecation window and a documented public API. Until then, pywire takes clean
              breaks over compatibility shims.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <span class="status after">After 1.0</span><h3>Polyglot islands</h3><p>
              Drop a React, Svelte or Vue component, or a web component, into a page. <code>pywire build</code> either runs
              the whole build or plugs into the one you have.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Discussion 28"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions/28"
                rel="external">Discussion #28</a
              ><a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Discussion 29"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions/29"
                rel="external">Discussion #29</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <span class="status after">After 1.0</span><h3>An agent UI add-on</h3><p>
              An official add-on for building AI features into your app: a chat panel whose agent can read the page,
              fill forms and click through flows with the user's permission, and rearrange the layout when asked.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>A plugin API</h3><p>
              Custom directives and hooks, turning today's <code>connect_*</code> pattern into a supported extension point.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Discussion 25"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions/25"
                rel="external">Discussion #25</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>Faster builds and pages</h3><p>
              Pages with nothing interactive prerendered at build time, image optimization, and constant folding in the
              compiler.
            </p><div class="meta">
              <a
                class="ref"
                aria-label="Discussion 27"
                href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions/27"
                rel="external">Discussion #27</a
              ><a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 154" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/154" rel="external"
                >#154</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>A profiler</h3><p>Time and memory for every lifecycle phase of a page, in the dev tools.</p><div
              class="meta"
            >
              <a class="ref" aria-label="Issue 13" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/issues/13" rel="external"
                >#13</a
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="item">
            <h3>More editors</h3><p>
              JetBrains, Neovim and Zed setups on top of the language server and tree-sitter grammar.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="item far">
            <span class="status after">After 1.0</span><h3>Desktop and mobile apps</h3><p>
              Native desktop and mobile apps from the same <code>.wire</code> code, growing out of the pywire-shell prototype.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="one" aria-labelledby="one-h">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="intro">
            <p class="eyebrow">Version 1.0</p>
            <h2 id="one-h">What 1.0 means</h2>
            <p>
              Two promises at once: an API that won't break under you, and a framework you can run a real product on.
            </p>
          </div>
          <ul class="checks">
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>No open correctness bugs in
              components, reactivity or forms
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>Every core feature works on
              server and edge, with a published capability table
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>Auth, security, testing and
              observability add-ons at 1.0 alongside the core
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>Real-time between users: shared
              state, presence and rooms
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>Live data queries and a settled
              FastAPI integration
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>A published stability policy,
              with docs and tutorial that match the API exactly
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>A reference SaaS app built on
              pywire
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="lucide:check" /></span>A handful of regular contributors
              beyond the maintainer
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="wrap two">
      <section class="card" aria-labelledby="how-h">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it gets built</p>
        <h2 id="how-h">One maintainer, a lot of agents</h2>
        <p>
          pywire has one maintainer, and AI coding agents write most of the code. Every change is reviewed by the
          maintainer and has to pass CI, including unit, client and browser end-to-end tests, before it merges. Agents
          are taking over routine fixes and speeding up feature work.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="card" aria-labelledby="shape-h">
        <p class="eyebrow">Get involved</p>
        <h2 id="shape-h">Shape what comes next</h2>
        <p>
          The order on this page moves with what people build. Say what you need in GitHub Discussions, or support the
          maintainer if pywire saves you time.
        </p>
        <div class="btns">
          <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/pywire/pywire/discussions" rel="external"
            >Join the discussion<Icon aria-hidden="true" name="lucide:arrow-right" /></a
          >
          <a class="btn ghost" href="https://ko-fi.com/reecelikesramen" rel="external">Support on Ko-fi</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/sponsors/pywire" rel="external">Sponsor on GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</DefaultLayout>

<style lang="scss" is:global>
  .roadmap-page {
    --rm-accent-strong: light-dark(oklch(48% 0.12 195), var(--color-wire-90));
    --rm-wire-dim: light-dark(oklch(80% 0.05 195), oklch(45% 0.07 195));
    --rm-btn-bg: light-dark(oklch(48% 0.12 195), var(--color-wire-90));
    --rm-btn-bg-hover: light-dark(oklch(42% 0.11 195), oklch(94% 0.1 195));
    --rm-btn-fg: light-dark(#fff, oklch(15% 0.04 195));
    --rm-glow:
      0 0 0 1px color-mix(in oklch, var(--accent-color) 30%, transparent),
      0 8px 24px color-mix(in oklch, var(--accent-color) 12%, transparent);
    --rm-gutter: clamp(1.125rem, 2.4vw, 2.5rem);

    h1,
    h2,
    h3 {
      margin: 0;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
    p {
      margin: 0;
      text-wrap: pretty;
    }
    ul {
      margin: 0;
    }
    code {
      border-radius: var(--radius-s);
      background: var(--accent-bg-color);
      padding: 0.05em 0.35em;
      color: var(--rm-accent-strong);
      font-size: 0.86em;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    a:focus-visible {
      outline: 3px solid var(--rm-accent-strong);
      outline-offset: 2px;
    }
    .wrap {
      margin-inline: auto;
      padding-inline: var(--rm-gutter);
      max-width: 1200px;
    }
    .eyebrow {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--rm-accent-strong);
      font-weight: 500;
      font-size: 0.76rem;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    [data-icon] {
      flex: none;
      width: 1em;
      height: 1em;
    }

    /* hero */
    .hero {
      position: relative;
      padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem) clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
      overflow: hidden;
    }
    .hero::before {
      position: absolute;
      z-index: -1;
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 70%, transparent);
      mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 70%, transparent);
      inset: 0;
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(var(--grid-line) 1px, transparent 1px),
        linear-gradient(90deg, var(--grid-line) 1px, transparent 1px),
        linear-gradient(var(--grid-line-strong) 1px, transparent 1px),
        linear-gradient(90deg, var(--grid-line-strong) 1px, transparent 1px);
      background-size:
        32px 32px,
        32px 32px,
        128px 128px,
        128px 128px;
      pointer-events: none;
      content: '';
    }
    .hero .wrap {
      display: grid;
      gap: 1.25rem;
    }
    .hero h1 {
      max-width: 14ch;
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 1.6rem + 4.6vw, 4.6rem);
      line-height: 1.03;
      letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    }
    .hero h1 span {
      color: var(--accent-color);
      text-shadow: 0 0 24px color-mix(in oklch, var(--accent-color) 28%, transparent);
    }
    .hero .lede {
      max-width: 40rem;
      color: var(--text-decoration-color);
      font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 0.95rem + 0.45vw, 1.28rem);
    }
    .legend {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0.5rem 1.4rem;
